1
00:00:08,745 --> 00:00:12,527
[SPEAKER_04]: Okay, so my question is about dukkha contemplation.

2
00:00:12,807 --> 00:00:34,457
[SPEAKER_04]: So, like, I encountered that, like, if there is not enough faith, then this dukkha contemplation can kind of cause depression, sort of, because, like, when you consider that, like, I mean, death is inevitable, suffering is inevitable,

3
00:00:34,995 --> 00:00:37,136
[SPEAKER_04]: And you don't know that there is a way out of it.

4
00:00:37,456 --> 00:00:41,138
[SPEAKER_04]: So, maybe, like, even your practice is also futile.

5
00:00:41,978 --> 00:00:46,960
[SPEAKER_04]: So, and basically, then, what you are, kind of, you are just stuck in suffering.

6
00:00:47,981 --> 00:00:52,263
[SPEAKER_04]: And, yeah, so, how to deal with this?

7
00:00:53,403 --> 00:01:02,167
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, I think, see, the first question would be, like, why would you be contemplating dukkha in the first place?

8
00:01:02,647 --> 00:01:04,248
[SPEAKER_00]: What's your motivation behind it?

9
00:01:06,589 --> 00:01:09,190
[SPEAKER_04]: Well, as far as I understand, it's...

10
00:01:09,911 --> 00:01:13,893
[SPEAKER_04]: I mean, I think it kind of makes you sober about life.

11
00:01:14,814 --> 00:01:15,995
[SPEAKER_00]: No, but I mean specifically you.

12
00:01:16,975 --> 00:01:19,677
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, when you contemplate dukkha, what's... why are you doing it?

13
00:01:19,817 --> 00:01:23,359
[SPEAKER_00]: What do you expect to come from that contemplation as a practice?

14
00:01:23,939 --> 00:01:28,362
[SPEAKER_04]: I would say I expect, like, some calmness and dispassion to come from it.

15
00:01:28,902 --> 00:01:34,606
[SPEAKER_04]: But it is often, instead of this, it's coming a kind of some kind of despair or sort of... yeah.

16
00:01:35,091 --> 00:01:39,113
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, see, I think that's already pretty much where the answer is.

17
00:01:39,994 --> 00:01:42,175
[SPEAKER_00]: You're doing it to get something from it.

18
00:01:43,595 --> 00:01:46,677
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you want to contemplate it to get the calmness, to get the dispassion.

19
00:01:47,958 --> 00:01:51,460
[SPEAKER_00]: But, really, you want to contemplate it so to understand it on its own terms.

20
00:01:52,820 --> 00:02:03,706
[SPEAKER_00]: Even if no calmness comes, if no dispassion comes, which, by the way, it's impossible for it to not come, but if your motivating factor is, I want these things to come, that's why I contemplate this,

21
00:02:04,153 --> 00:02:07,774
[SPEAKER_00]: That can kind of take the center stage too much.

22
00:02:07,795 --> 00:02:20,640
[SPEAKER_00]: So, although theoretically, yes, you contemplate it, so to be dispassionate, but on the immediate level, your immediate motivation can be the reason why you're not getting what you're expecting to get.

23
00:02:21,840 --> 00:02:32,184
[SPEAKER_00]: In other words, the craving because of which you're contemplating that, is maintained because you're overlooking it and feeling justified in craving further of this contemplation until the peace and dispassion come.

24
00:02:34,062 --> 00:02:36,643
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you're kind of blocking yourself, so to speak.

25
00:02:37,923 --> 00:02:44,545
[SPEAKER_04]: But I'd say, in my case, it's even not quite like this, because it seems like this contemplation is running in my head on their own.

26
00:02:45,085 --> 00:02:47,585
[SPEAKER_04]: Like, eh... to...

27
00:02:48,385 --> 00:02:50,246
[SPEAKER_04]: I know, it's almost automatically.

28
00:02:50,346 --> 00:02:52,066
[SPEAKER_04]: It is because, eh...

29
00:02:52,386 --> 00:02:58,268
[SPEAKER_04]: I noticed that in past, for instance, these things helped me to overcome, like, attachment to many things.

30
00:02:58,288 --> 00:03:03,189
[SPEAKER_04]: Like, to see, for instance, that work is not really, like, so much important, that, I don't know, like...

31
00:03:03,488 --> 00:03:10,311
[SPEAKER_04]: Well, that's... again, like, even back then, all of those contemplations were infected with the craving, with the desire of wanting to get something from it.

32
00:03:10,511 --> 00:03:13,613
[SPEAKER_04]: And it can work, relatively speaking, to some extent.

33
00:03:33,860 --> 00:03:44,102
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, that craving can help you replace, you know, craving for family, partnership, work, career, to crave for forest, meditation, and so on.

34
00:03:44,582 --> 00:03:45,462
[SPEAKER_00]: But it's still craving.

35
00:03:45,662 --> 00:03:50,323
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the sooner or later, the time will come when that will be the impediment, the obstacle.

36
00:03:51,383 --> 00:04:01,005
[SPEAKER_00]: And, given that, yes, you already have no desire for career, or work, or academic pursuits, or family, or partnerships, what you're left with now is

37
00:04:01,620 --> 00:04:10,204
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, that craving that has helped you overcome those, say, worldly attachments, coarser attachments, now it's just too coarse to address the subtler attachments.

38
00:04:11,205 --> 00:04:12,085
[SPEAKER_00]: And what's too coarse?

39
00:04:12,225 --> 00:04:14,046
[SPEAKER_00]: It's that very craving that motivates.

40
00:04:14,746 --> 00:04:20,189
[SPEAKER_00]: Those expectations on account of contemplation, I expect peace and dispassion, but it's not coming.

41
00:04:20,229 --> 00:04:21,629
[SPEAKER_00]: It's just depression and despair.

42
00:04:22,910 --> 00:04:25,991
[SPEAKER_00]: Because craving keeps that, as I said, polluted.

43
00:04:27,072 --> 00:04:29,193
[SPEAKER_00]: So you really want to, like, recalibrate.

44
00:04:29,730 --> 00:04:33,892
[SPEAKER_00]: Pretty much the context and the reasons for contemplating things in the first place and whereby...

45
00:04:34,432 --> 00:04:34,772
[SPEAKER_00]: Hold on.

46
00:04:35,753 --> 00:04:41,335
[SPEAKER_00]: Would I be fine if for the rest of my life I don't experience any peace nor any dispassion?

47
00:04:41,596 --> 00:04:42,716
[SPEAKER_00]: Why is that a problem for me?

48
00:04:43,336 --> 00:04:45,397
[SPEAKER_00]: I still know what to do and what not to do.

49
00:04:46,718 --> 00:04:53,041
[SPEAKER_00]: It still doesn't affect my choice to keep the precepts and choose wisely, even if I don't get any reward for it.

50
00:04:53,761 --> 00:04:57,083
[SPEAKER_00]: So you want to present that idea and see whether your mind will be like, whoa, whoa.

51
00:04:57,695 --> 00:04:58,315
[SPEAKER_00]: Not a chance.

52
00:04:58,375 --> 00:04:59,235
[SPEAKER_00]: And it's like, well, why not?

53
00:04:59,895 --> 00:05:00,736
[SPEAKER_00]: Because it could happen.

54
00:05:02,156 --> 00:05:07,857
[SPEAKER_00]: Maybe you lose your health or something and you won't be able to practice all these things that you deem practice to be.

55
00:05:09,318 --> 00:05:12,518
[SPEAKER_00]: But internally, theoretically, you shouldn't be affected by that.

56
00:05:13,338 --> 00:05:15,519
[SPEAKER_00]: If you're practicing rightly, you shouldn't be affected by that.

57
00:05:15,839 --> 00:05:21,900
[SPEAKER_00]: So that's what you want to start showing to your own mind that, well, the time will come when we won't be able to manage or do anything.

58
00:05:23,301 --> 00:05:24,421
[SPEAKER_00]: Will you be fine with that?

59
00:05:25,300 --> 00:05:38,629
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's what I mean, like, you want to kind of refine your own motivation for practice, whereby... No, I contemplate dukkha, so... to understand it, and so that my mind gets used to it, that there is no resistance or depression in face of it.

60
00:05:39,870 --> 00:05:41,331
[SPEAKER_00]: That's a reward in and of itself.

61
00:05:41,991 --> 00:05:47,715
[SPEAKER_00]: But as of now, that reward is too subtle to be measured by the expectation of reward, of peace and dispassion.

62
00:05:48,716 --> 00:05:50,357
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's why it feels insignificant.

63
00:05:58,473 --> 00:06:12,663
[SPEAKER_04]: So then, what should one kind of tell to the mind, tell to oneself, that it stops expecting this kind of

64
00:06:13,751 --> 00:06:43,337
[SPEAKER_04]: I know, it's basically saying, okay, like, you're doing this, you're keeping precepts, or you're doing your meditation, your contemplation, or whatever else, but you still, you feel like you're still afraid of suffering, still afraid of death, of, like, I mean, let's say, on, like, sometimes mind presents some, like, violent scenarios, which are, kind of, disturbing, yeah, frightening, and, and you see, like,

65
00:06:43,845 --> 00:06:44,866
[SPEAKER_04]: I know what to do with this.

66
00:06:45,246 --> 00:06:45,566
[SPEAKER_04]: Yeah.

67
00:06:46,707 --> 00:06:58,274
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, again, for as long as... See, the mind can only go so far if your views don't support mind's expectations.

68
00:06:59,255 --> 00:07:06,279
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it feels as if, oh, well, my mind still expects, or my mind fears, or my mind does this, I must address it.

69
00:07:06,679 --> 00:07:09,141
[SPEAKER_00]: But all of that is enabled by your views.

70
00:07:10,086 --> 00:07:12,807
[SPEAKER_00]: By what you still emphasize or deem important.

71
00:07:13,727 --> 00:07:16,107
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if you start rewriting your views, i.e.

72
00:07:16,187 --> 00:07:19,488
[SPEAKER_00]: correcting them, then the mind can't go that far.

73
00:07:20,868 --> 00:07:22,528
[SPEAKER_00]: And, eventually, it will have to start to follow.

74
00:07:22,548 --> 00:07:24,789
[SPEAKER_00]: Because the views direct, they set up the direction.

75
00:07:25,889 --> 00:07:36,071
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if your mind is still expecting, it still means you, yourself, is a good chance, are also still expecting it on account of the views of the practice and what's important and what should come from it.

76
00:07:36,111 --> 00:07:37,551
[SPEAKER_00]: You haven't questioned that long enough.

77
00:07:38,365 --> 00:07:52,790
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, and by the way, like, maybe part of your views might be quite okay, quite right, but the fact that they were never questioned, never brought to question and into uncertainty for a sufficient amount of time, they are wrong for that reason.

78
00:07:54,990 --> 00:07:59,812
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why, like, even the right view, people can be holding it wrongly, which is why it won't be their right view.

79
00:08:02,294 --> 00:08:05,237
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you yourself have to be re-evaluating yourself.

80
00:08:05,917 --> 00:08:08,279
[SPEAKER_00]: It's like, okay, but am I also expecting things?

81
00:08:08,519 --> 00:08:14,645
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, contemplate enough to show you, until it sinks in, that those things shouldn't be expected, because they make no sense.

82
00:08:15,405 --> 00:08:22,872
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, even if the mind comes up with expectations, it's contained within the right perception that you developed, with more correct views.

83
00:08:24,953 --> 00:08:30,318
[SPEAKER_00]: So, ask yourself, as I said, eh... Would you be fine?

84
00:08:31,927 --> 00:08:42,254
[SPEAKER_00]: With uncomfortable things, not being able to practice, or be on a retreat, or be in a seclusion, or be sheltered from stuff, if you end up in an environment where those things are not accessible.

85
00:08:43,235 --> 00:08:46,397
[SPEAKER_00]: Now, in your imagination, is that affecting you?

86
00:08:48,698 --> 00:08:51,280
[SPEAKER_00]: And if it is, you realize, well, that's really the root of the problem.

87
00:08:54,623 --> 00:08:56,464
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's already the first step towards solution.

88
00:08:56,625 --> 00:09:05,171
[SPEAKER_00]: Not just taking for granted that, yeah, yeah, I must follow this through, whereby I will get that environment, will get that practice, and never address the fact that it bothers me.

89
00:09:08,033 --> 00:09:12,076
[SPEAKER_04]: And if I figure out that I'm bothered by it, so what's... Well, that's... Let's see.

90
00:09:12,136 --> 00:09:16,800
[SPEAKER_00]: You figure out that you're bothered by it, and you recognize, okay, this is what needs to be addressed.

91
00:09:16,900 --> 00:09:22,284
[SPEAKER_00]: And whenever the pressure comes up to follow that which bothers you, as in, like, to avoid it,

92
00:09:23,001 --> 00:09:25,462
[SPEAKER_00]: To go away with it, it's like, well, actually, no, that would be fueling it.

93
00:09:28,324 --> 00:09:38,429
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's how you can, as I said, find a good enough balance in terms of environment, where you wouldn't be too precious or too, like, high maintenance, so to speak.

94
00:09:38,509 --> 00:09:42,772
[SPEAKER_00]: And, well, the first step of when you find it your bother is to not act out of the bother.

95
00:09:44,453 --> 00:09:48,355
[SPEAKER_00]: That's how the views that revolve around management of the bother, avoiding the bother,

96
00:09:49,267 --> 00:09:51,669
[SPEAKER_00]: Finding the perfect environment where nothing will bother you.

97
00:09:51,689 --> 00:09:53,510
[SPEAKER_00]: Those views will then evaporate.

98
00:09:54,850 --> 00:10:10,900
[SPEAKER_00]: Because, again, for maintenance of the views, the whole kind of... Well, the views would pretty much depend on mind jumping on things, left and right, management, this, avoidance, resistance.

99
00:10:11,000 --> 00:10:16,023
[SPEAKER_00]: Mind jumping left and right, management, avoidance, resistance, depends on you enabling it through your actions.

100
00:10:16,990 --> 00:10:21,652
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if you start not following through with your actions, the mind will have to come down.

101
00:10:22,913 --> 00:10:26,034
[SPEAKER_00]: If the mind comes down, the views would have to settle down.

102
00:10:27,294 --> 00:10:32,597
[SPEAKER_00]: They can't remain, the cloud of dust can't remain if there is nothing running up and down the road anymore.

103
00:10:34,217 --> 00:10:42,261
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's not like instantaneously kind of unplugging something, but it is like removing the supporting factor until it settles down.

104
00:10:44,490 --> 00:11:00,160
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's what we say, like, it kind of boils down to recognizing the motivation for whatever aspect of the practice you think you're developing or you want to be developing, and then not taking that motivation for granted either, but recognizing, hold on, is this rooted in avoidance?

105
00:11:01,640 --> 00:11:02,641
[SPEAKER_00]: Is this rooted in fear?

106
00:11:02,721 --> 00:11:05,223
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, am I addressing fear or I'm just trying to not deal with it?

107
00:11:07,224 --> 00:11:12,087
[SPEAKER_00]: And start, like, bringing it back home, basically, where you kind of know, I should not be...

108
00:11:12,107 --> 00:11:13,768
[SPEAKER_00]: I should be able to not be moved by this.

109
00:11:14,787 --> 00:11:23,368
[SPEAKER_00]: Even if my mind moves, I'm the one who should be able to not just act out and follow through with these pressures and compulsions and so on.

110
00:11:55,419 --> 00:12:10,313
[SPEAKER_04]: So then, I'd say, the start here is to kind of stop trying to find some like perfect environment for practice and whatever.

111
00:12:10,833 --> 00:12:12,194
[SPEAKER_00]: A hundred percent.

112
00:12:12,214 --> 00:12:12,795
[SPEAKER_00]: A hundred percent.

113
00:12:13,676 --> 00:12:19,661
[SPEAKER_00]: And like, the sooner you stop that, the sooner you begin the practice.

114
00:12:21,387 --> 00:12:28,891
[SPEAKER_00]: The practice that really matters towards actual liability to suffer and everything else, regardless of the circumstances.

115
00:12:29,312 --> 00:12:44,860
[SPEAKER_00]: Because the very kind of... the nature of, say, consciously choosing to not follow the compulsion to manage or address the discomfort that arises, it's going to feel like a very strong confinement.

116
00:12:46,681 --> 00:12:47,682
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's why people don't do it.

117
00:12:48,454 --> 00:12:53,698
[SPEAKER_00]: That's what they justified trying, like, changing, and going further, and doing this, and doing that, and moving from here to there.

118
00:12:54,078 --> 00:12:57,801
[SPEAKER_00]: Because if they stop, it feels like you're enclosed completely.

119
00:12:58,462 --> 00:13:00,804
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, like, no, no escape.

120
00:13:02,285 --> 00:13:03,046
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's unpleasant.

121
00:13:03,166 --> 00:13:04,427
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's the beginning of practice.

122
00:13:06,348 --> 00:13:13,734
[SPEAKER_00]: So that's why, again, the precepts and everything is just to train the mind to enable you to be able to contain your mind when it faces that confinement.

123
00:13:15,117 --> 00:13:19,560
[SPEAKER_00]: And that confinement will come, as the Buddha described, the four mountains coming closer with every day.

124
00:13:20,420 --> 00:13:25,063
[SPEAKER_00]: It's nothing other than the confinement, until you're confined so much that you're destroyed.

125
00:13:26,865 --> 00:13:42,655
[SPEAKER_00]: Sickness, aging, death are the unavoidable confinements, but the whole practice is to create the confinement of the right kind, so that the mind learns how to settle within, not to depend on action and management

126
00:13:43,175 --> 00:13:45,196
[SPEAKER_00]: In order for its perception of its survival.

127
00:13:47,097 --> 00:13:55,480
[SPEAKER_00]: So, in other words, you want to do it before... You want to train your mind in regards to confinement before the confinement really comes.

128
00:13:55,540 --> 00:13:57,621
[SPEAKER_00]: But, yes, psychologically, it will feel the same.

129
00:13:57,661 --> 00:14:03,883
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, for many people, just having to take on the precepts will feel like death, dying, fear, abyss, as the suttas describe.

130
00:14:04,664 --> 00:14:06,685
[SPEAKER_00]: Because sensuality is that freedom.

131
00:14:07,765 --> 00:14:10,106
[SPEAKER_00]: Saying no to that feels like...

132
00:14:10,146 --> 00:14:12,387
[SPEAKER_00]: I mean, like you're buried into the ground alive.

133
00:14:13,564 --> 00:14:17,285
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's... that's frightening, but only from the point of view of depending on the senses.

134
00:14:18,165 --> 00:14:25,167
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you might not have... say, you might have refined that, so you don't depend on the senses as much, because you've been restrained, living in the right environments, and so on.

135
00:14:25,587 --> 00:14:33,770
[SPEAKER_00]: But you might well be managing... depending on management, on technique, or counteraction, when this happens and that, on movement within.

136
00:14:35,350 --> 00:14:37,971
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's also a way of escaping confinement.

137
00:14:39,157 --> 00:14:40,797
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's what the Buddha said, like in the suttas.

138
00:14:40,817 --> 00:14:48,339
[SPEAKER_00]: He says, you have Samana and Brahmins that do take on restraint and start living withdrawn from sensuality.

139
00:14:48,839 --> 00:14:53,960
[SPEAKER_00]: But then they hold on to the views and start arguing and falling out over views.

140
00:14:55,220 --> 00:15:00,442
[SPEAKER_00]: People often think that it's like philosophical debates that they disagree with.

141
00:15:00,522 --> 00:15:01,482
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, the world is eternal.

142
00:15:01,542 --> 00:15:02,722
[SPEAKER_00]: No, the world is not eternal.

143
00:15:02,762 --> 00:15:06,443
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, you, sir, don't know what you talk about and stuff like... No, views literally means

144
00:15:07,682 --> 00:15:14,888
[SPEAKER_00]: Standpoints in regard to day-to-day life will become supercharged with importance once you withdraw yourself from centrality.

145
00:15:16,409 --> 00:15:25,596
[SPEAKER_00]: Views that range, and everybody that lived in the monastery saw that, that range from, sure, is the world eternal or not, to the point of, how did you put your shoes when you came in here?

146
00:15:25,876 --> 00:15:27,197
[SPEAKER_00]: In this monastery, we don't do that.

147
00:15:28,559 --> 00:15:29,920
[SPEAKER_00]: And it's like, it's really important.

148
00:15:30,575 --> 00:15:39,285
[SPEAKER_00]: Because confinement has come now, because you are not spilling out into the senses, so you're going to have different ways internally of compensating for that fear.

149
00:15:40,326 --> 00:15:47,875
[SPEAKER_00]: So these little things that other people do become very, very important and must be aligned rightly, because otherwise, I feel confined.

150
00:15:48,874 --> 00:16:02,841
[SPEAKER_00]: And if I don't give in the first wave of compulsion to break out of that internal mental confinement of my citta, then I will give in to the second wave, or the third wave, because they're going to become more and more increasingly pressuring and irrational.

151
00:16:04,322 --> 00:16:05,983
[SPEAKER_00]: And that was the example many of us seen.

152
00:16:06,043 --> 00:16:15,308
[SPEAKER_00]: It's like, you know, the suttas clearly say you shouldn't say anything if it's rooted in irritation or anger, until your mind is in the right place, until you recognize that it's beneficial for everybody.

153
00:16:15,868 --> 00:16:16,769
[SPEAKER_00]: And you would see that, like,

154
00:16:17,241 --> 00:16:23,786
[SPEAKER_00]: Something would come up in the community, and you could see the monkeys, like, super irritated, annoyed, threatened by it, even.

155
00:16:24,787 --> 00:16:34,555
[SPEAKER_00]: And they might not say anything that immediate occasion, but the next occasion, they would have to say something, because in their mind, it's like the proliferation increases.

156
00:16:35,195 --> 00:16:38,798
[SPEAKER_00]: So, oh, if I don't say like this, he'll do it again.

157
00:16:40,340 --> 00:16:43,322
[SPEAKER_00]: They don't stop and question, well, what if they do it for the rest of their life?

158
00:16:44,637 --> 00:16:47,319
[SPEAKER_00]: What if everybody does the wrong thing for the rest of their life?

159
00:16:48,099 --> 00:16:50,481
[SPEAKER_00]: Can I endure that confinement of not getting my way?

160
00:16:50,621 --> 00:16:52,062
[SPEAKER_00]: And it's like, they don't go that far.

161
00:16:52,102 --> 00:16:56,385
[SPEAKER_00]: It's like, I might not say the first day, but then I'll think, oh, but he'll do it again.

162
00:16:57,185 --> 00:16:59,147
[SPEAKER_00]: But then, if he does it, then he'll do it as well.

163
00:17:00,263 --> 00:17:00,843
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, she'll do it.

164
00:17:00,903 --> 00:17:01,944
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, everybody will do it.

165
00:17:02,104 --> 00:17:08,208
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, everything I worked for, the monastery I built, the tradition we preserved, all of it will go to ruin.

166
00:17:08,408 --> 00:17:09,369
[SPEAKER_00]: Therefore, I have to say it.

167
00:17:09,689 --> 00:17:12,031
[SPEAKER_00]: For the sake of the dhamma, for the sake of the monastery, for the sake...

168
00:17:12,051 --> 00:17:12,671
[SPEAKER_00]: I have to say it.

169
00:17:13,111 --> 00:17:23,558
[SPEAKER_00]: So, they then prioritize proliferated ideas of external importance and circumstances and ignore their own very intention that they're still annoyed and that's why they're saying it.

170
00:17:26,350 --> 00:17:39,895
[SPEAKER_00]: And, like, you say no once to your mind, you might feel a bit of, like, a resistance, but it won't become increasingly or intensely irrational immediately, because, again, there is still hope that eventually you'll say it, and the mind will get its way.

171
00:17:40,675 --> 00:17:48,198
[SPEAKER_00]: But if you don't say it again, and again, and again, the confinement, the perception of confinement, is increasing, and the animal is going to start complaining.

172
00:17:48,998 --> 00:17:53,741
[SPEAKER_00]: It's going to be quicker to be agitated, more irrational, more pressuring, more threatening.

173
00:17:53,861 --> 00:17:55,002
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's exactly the point.

174
00:17:55,042 --> 00:17:56,103
[SPEAKER_00]: That's part of the practice.

175
00:17:57,043 --> 00:18:00,566
[SPEAKER_00]: But once that starts happening, that's where people start to justify spilling out.

176
00:18:01,426 --> 00:18:06,570
[SPEAKER_00]: So they might not spill out as much as go back to the sensuality, but it will certainly spill out to the point of

177
00:18:07,309 --> 00:18:14,613
[SPEAKER_00]: Arguing, carrying grudges, ill will on account of views and disagreements, and how things should be, and how things should run, and what you said, and what he meant, and so on.

178
00:18:15,273 --> 00:18:22,877
[SPEAKER_00]: And all of those are views on account of which samanas and brahmins stab each other with verbal daggers, as the suttas describe.

179
00:18:23,758 --> 00:18:26,139
[SPEAKER_00]: And then you realize, oh, yeah, okay, so I shouldn't do that either.

180
00:18:27,219 --> 00:18:31,141
[SPEAKER_00]: So I have withdrawn myself from sensuality, but now I have to withdraw myself from these standpoints.

181
00:18:31,662 --> 00:18:32,362
[SPEAKER_00]: How do I do that?

182
00:18:32,422 --> 00:18:33,963
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, by questioning them for myself.

183
00:18:35,320 --> 00:18:36,461
[SPEAKER_00]: How is that going to feel?

184
00:18:36,541 --> 00:18:38,062
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, exactly how it felt before.

185
00:18:38,102 --> 00:18:49,449
[SPEAKER_00]: It's gonna feel like your mind is now even more confined, because you're not allowing it quick escape, quick management, quick cover-up, quick kind of addressing of things, so that everything feel fine, feels fine.

186
00:18:51,330 --> 00:18:53,331
[SPEAKER_00]: And and that's that's actually a natural progression.

187
00:18:54,172 --> 00:19:04,358
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's why I say, like, you choose to restrain today, and then in five days time, there's still the same pressure to say something comes up, and you still restrain.

188
00:19:05,577 --> 00:19:08,419
[SPEAKER_00]: And then you restrain to the point of there is no expiry date to this.

189
00:19:09,099 --> 00:19:17,604
[SPEAKER_00]: If the mind keeps coming up for the rest of my life with this ridiculous, irrational, compulsion, pressure, for the rest of my life I will not intentionally allow it.

190
00:19:18,304 --> 00:19:20,526
[SPEAKER_00]: And then the mind will get really, really upset with you.

191
00:19:22,107 --> 00:19:25,349
[SPEAKER_00]: Because now we're talking about, kind of, ultimate scenarios.

192
00:19:26,249 --> 00:19:30,752
[SPEAKER_00]: You're taking away the hope that sooner or later you will give in, like how you used to do.

193
00:19:31,564 --> 00:19:32,004
[SPEAKER_00]: But you don't.

194
00:19:32,425 --> 00:19:41,513
[SPEAKER_00]: And by the way, it doesn't need to be this kind of grandiose determinations of, I will never lay down or I shall never speak.

195
00:19:42,294 --> 00:19:43,395
[SPEAKER_00]: Just these little things.

196
00:19:44,156 --> 00:19:50,642
[SPEAKER_00]: When the, I don't know, the eyes want to look, the mouth wants to say something, you recognize it's unwholesome, you don't do it.

197
00:19:52,083 --> 00:19:53,865
[SPEAKER_00]: And then it comes up again and again.

198
00:19:53,925 --> 00:19:56,287
[SPEAKER_00]: And three months later, the same thing keeps coming up.

199
00:19:57,453 --> 00:19:57,893
[SPEAKER_00]: You don't know.

200
00:19:58,134 --> 00:20:03,537
[SPEAKER_00]: It might be three days, it might be three months, it might be three years, because you don't know how deep the tendencies of citta go.

201
00:20:04,378 --> 00:20:11,463
[SPEAKER_00]: You are ready to train the animal, and if you are ready to train the animal, it doesn't matter to you how long you have to train it.

202
00:20:11,543 --> 00:20:14,465
[SPEAKER_00]: It would be nice, sure, if it's not too long, but doesn't matter.

203
00:20:14,705 --> 00:20:19,809
[SPEAKER_00]: You're not concerned with the length, you're concerned with the quality of each and every session, so to speak.

204
00:20:21,428 --> 00:20:28,410
[SPEAKER_00]: And what preferences your citta have, well, that can... and how deep they go, well, that can only become apparent as you start training it rightly.

205
00:20:30,430 --> 00:20:35,352
[SPEAKER_00]: Because sometimes people would think, well, surely, two months later, I shouldn't be thinking about this same pressure.

206
00:20:35,372 --> 00:20:36,532
[SPEAKER_00]: It was like, why not?

207
00:20:38,292 --> 00:20:39,133
[SPEAKER_00]: Are you thinking it?

208
00:20:39,993 --> 00:20:40,953
[SPEAKER_00]: Are you choosing to think it?

209
00:20:40,973 --> 00:20:41,913
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, no, not really.

210
00:20:42,634 --> 00:20:45,654
[SPEAKER_00]: So, why do you then expect to not be thinking about it?

211
00:20:45,814 --> 00:20:47,275
[SPEAKER_00]: Is that also up to you to decide?

212
00:20:47,295 --> 00:20:47,595
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, no.

213
00:20:48,298 --> 00:20:51,520
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if for two years later my mind is pressuring me with same stuff, that's fine.

214
00:20:52,101 --> 00:20:55,723
[SPEAKER_00]: I was never... the practice was never about changing what mind thinks about.

215
00:20:57,024 --> 00:21:00,286
[SPEAKER_00]: The practice was not crossing the line when those things want to push you over.

216
00:21:01,727 --> 00:21:08,072
[SPEAKER_00]: And if the mind... what the mind thinks about are not the things that want to cross the line in terms of unbeneficial behavior, that's fine.

217
00:21:09,753 --> 00:21:14,116
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's how you also recognize kind of where your responsibility is.

218
00:21:17,528 --> 00:21:30,795
[SPEAKER_00]: So, not spilling into sensuality, not spilling into coarse act by body and speech, and then, yeah, not finding the escape from that confinement in views and standpoints and ideas of how things should be and so on and so forth.

219
00:21:32,396 --> 00:21:35,637
[SPEAKER_00]: And when the mind does come up with those expectations, you recognize the same.

220
00:21:35,757 --> 00:21:42,541
[SPEAKER_00]: Ah, I have to endure this now and not fuel it and not take it for granted and, okay, yeah, yeah, yeah, let's find a solution, let's sort it out.

221
00:21:42,701 --> 00:21:43,201
[SPEAKER_00]: No, hold on.

222
00:21:44,382 --> 00:21:45,242
[SPEAKER_00]: Why is it a problem?

223
00:21:46,978 --> 00:21:53,163
[SPEAKER_00]: Why does anything, like, anything that mind comes up with, why do I have to do anything about it?

224
00:21:53,963 --> 00:21:54,544
[SPEAKER_00]: Anything about it?

225
00:21:54,564 --> 00:21:55,344
[SPEAKER_00]: Why do I have to move?

226
00:21:55,365 --> 00:21:56,265
[SPEAKER_00]: Why do I have to figure it out?

227
00:21:56,285 --> 00:21:58,027
[SPEAKER_00]: Why do I... Why?

228
00:21:58,447 --> 00:22:00,869
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, why can't I just not do anything?

229
00:22:06,773 --> 00:22:10,556
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, you choose to do not do anything, and then the mind becomes even more threatening.

230
00:22:10,736 --> 00:22:12,057
[SPEAKER_00]: Not always, but it can happen.

231
00:22:14,379 --> 00:22:16,761
[SPEAKER_00]: And it's like, okay, well, surely that's the problem.

232
00:22:17,387 --> 00:22:19,828
[SPEAKER_00]: Not my doing or not doing of practice.

233
00:22:21,008 --> 00:22:28,070
[SPEAKER_00]: It's the fact that if the mind is not getting what it wants, when it wants it, it turns... it's turning against me.

234
00:22:28,610 --> 00:22:29,330
[SPEAKER_00]: It's pressuring me.

235
00:22:29,370 --> 00:22:30,790
[SPEAKER_00]: It's threatening me.

236
00:22:30,810 --> 00:22:31,930
[SPEAKER_00]: I'm the one like, oh...

237
00:22:32,751 --> 00:22:36,312
[SPEAKER_00]: It's like, well, that's... that's not a beneficial arrangement.

238
00:22:40,593 --> 00:22:46,674
[SPEAKER_00]: That the mind pressures you into decisions, into action, that you choose to give in because the pressure is too much.

239
00:23:02,670 --> 00:23:16,494
[SPEAKER_04]: But when, like, the doubt comes then, like, actually, mind starts saying, like, basically, it's there, kind of, you started this, because you thought that this can help you to, like, to overcome suffering.

240
00:23:17,134 --> 00:23:19,435
[SPEAKER_04]: Like, you could say, this is, this was craving.

241
00:23:19,975 --> 00:23:28,457
[SPEAKER_04]: But the mind starts saying, okay, you, you are going this way, maybe, like, I don't know, ten years or whatever, how many years, and you're still kind of like this.

242
00:23:28,497 --> 00:23:30,678
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, I mean, yeah, that's, that's a valid thing.

243
00:23:31,813 --> 00:23:33,234
[SPEAKER_00]: That's a valid thing for a mind to say.

244
00:23:33,955 --> 00:23:34,455
[SPEAKER_04]: Yes.

245
00:23:34,495 --> 00:23:36,397
[SPEAKER_00]: Because you should expect results.

246
00:23:36,617 --> 00:23:37,137
[SPEAKER_04]: Yes, yes.

247
00:23:37,317 --> 00:23:39,980
[SPEAKER_04]: And then you kind of say, maybe this, like, maybe it doesn't work.

248
00:23:40,060 --> 00:23:41,521
[SPEAKER_00]: Maybe... Or maybe you've been doing it wrong.

249
00:23:41,701 --> 00:23:42,662
[SPEAKER_04]: Maybe I've been doing it wrong.

250
00:23:42,682 --> 00:23:42,982
[SPEAKER_04]: Exactly.

251
00:23:43,002 --> 00:23:46,365
[SPEAKER_04]: Like, you start thinking, like, maybe I'm incapable of this.

252
00:23:46,485 --> 00:23:47,426
[SPEAKER_04]: Maybe it's just wrong.

253
00:23:47,526 --> 00:23:48,366
[SPEAKER_04]: Or maybe, like... Yeah.

254
00:23:48,506 --> 00:23:53,851
[SPEAKER_00]: So, there is a difference between, like, questioning it to an objective degree.

255
00:23:54,051 --> 00:23:55,972
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, well, yeah, I've been doing this for 10 years.

256
00:23:56,033 --> 00:23:56,453
[SPEAKER_00]: And, you know...

257
00:23:57,497 --> 00:24:08,206
[SPEAKER_00]: You don't have anything to show for, but then there is a difference between that and when you cross that line and go into, well, maybe you are just hopeless, maybe you don't know what you're doing, maybe...

258
00:24:08,386 --> 00:24:13,891
[SPEAKER_00]: It's like, no, the possibility for enlightenment remains undiminished for as long as you're alive.

259
00:24:16,113 --> 00:24:18,956
[SPEAKER_00]: But even this is questionable, you know.

260
00:24:19,276 --> 00:24:20,056
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, no, it's not.

261
00:24:20,117 --> 00:24:21,237
[SPEAKER_00]: Possibility is something...

262
00:24:21,378 --> 00:24:24,841
[SPEAKER_00]: It is possible that you can free yourself from suffering.

263
00:24:25,830 --> 00:24:35,775
[SPEAKER_00]: But sometimes, my doubt... Well, of course, of course, but that's, that's the, like... To a degree, you have to doubt things, including your own practice.

264
00:24:36,715 --> 00:24:43,659
[SPEAKER_00]: But when you recognize the doubt, now, to be revolving around, like, a compulsive core, that's when you have to endure it instead.

265
00:24:45,060 --> 00:24:51,523
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's the difference between, like, the doubt as a hindrance and the overall doubt in, like, what freedom from suffering is.

266
00:24:53,556 --> 00:24:58,860
[SPEAKER_00]: Because doubt is a hindrance, is a habit of the mind to pressure you to clarify things that are uncertain.

267
00:25:00,301 --> 00:25:05,385
[SPEAKER_00]: That's something you actually have to not give in to and habitually fuel it for the solution.

268
00:25:07,987 --> 00:25:10,809
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if the mind starts doubting, oh, well, is it even possible?

269
00:25:10,909 --> 00:25:12,370
[SPEAKER_00]: It's like, okay, that's a valid question.

270
00:25:13,271 --> 00:25:14,632
[SPEAKER_00]: But you stop there.

271
00:25:16,333 --> 00:25:17,113
[SPEAKER_00]: Is it even possible?

272
00:25:17,153 --> 00:25:18,735
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, I don't know, but I hope it is.

273
00:25:20,438 --> 00:25:23,322
[SPEAKER_00]: But that certainly doesn't allow you to... oh, yeah, you're worthless.

274
00:25:23,342 --> 00:25:24,464
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, where did that come from?

275
00:25:25,084 --> 00:25:26,586
[SPEAKER_00]: How do you know that that's the case?

276
00:25:26,827 --> 00:25:27,207
[SPEAKER_00]: You don't.

277
00:25:29,871 --> 00:25:36,039
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you might think like, oh, I'm nowhere near close to enlightenment, but how do you know that you are further away from it?

278
00:25:36,080 --> 00:25:36,941
[SPEAKER_00]: You don't know that either.

279
00:25:38,312 --> 00:25:47,595
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you recognize the objective uncertainty about it, but you have to be careful as to how much you allow it, that compulsion, to be fed through your own seeking for the solution.

280
00:25:47,675 --> 00:25:57,117
[SPEAKER_00]: Because, basically, when the doubt comes up as a hindrance, like repetitively, in that compulsive sense, it's not really about the answer.

281
00:25:57,197 --> 00:25:59,958
[SPEAKER_00]: It's about getting rid of the discomfort of that experience.

282
00:26:00,975 --> 00:26:03,918
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you're seeking the answer, seemingly, to clarify the doubt.

283
00:26:04,438 --> 00:26:10,024
[SPEAKER_00]: In reality, you're seeking the answer so that the mind can escape the confinement of this uncomfortable feeling.

284
00:26:10,184 --> 00:26:10,525
[SPEAKER_00]: That's it.

285
00:26:11,425 --> 00:26:20,074
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if you don't forget the doubt is unpleasant, and then make the effort to not act out of that displeasure, you are containing the doubt.

286
00:26:23,037 --> 00:26:24,118
[SPEAKER_00]: And the more you do it, like...

287
00:26:25,218 --> 00:26:26,559
[SPEAKER_00]: The more irrational it will become.

288
00:26:26,639 --> 00:26:30,920
[SPEAKER_00]: And the more irrational it becomes, it will show you more and more how ridiculous the mind is.

289
00:26:32,780 --> 00:26:35,661
[SPEAKER_00]: But it was the power of compulsion that will always get you back in.

290
00:26:37,101 --> 00:26:41,542
[SPEAKER_00]: And then become concerned with A's and B's and C's as the answers and all this.

291
00:26:41,582 --> 00:26:44,343
[SPEAKER_00]: But in reality, it was always remaining the same.

292
00:26:44,403 --> 00:26:45,763
[SPEAKER_00]: Confinement of discomfort.

293
00:26:46,623 --> 00:26:47,224
[SPEAKER_00]: Can't bear it.

294
00:26:47,404 --> 00:26:52,785
[SPEAKER_00]: Act out and then spend days and days trying to resolve something until the feeling is appeased.

295
00:26:55,453 --> 00:26:58,954
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's basically inadvertently fueling the doubt that you're trying to resolve.

296
00:27:01,816 --> 00:27:10,859
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if sometimes that doubt can have, as I said, a legitimate basis, such as, well, I've been doing this for 10 years, am I free from suffering?

297
00:27:10,919 --> 00:27:11,099
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

298
00:27:11,540 --> 00:27:12,760
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, that's a valid reflection.

299
00:27:13,560 --> 00:27:19,423
[SPEAKER_00]: But now, in the compulsion of that discomfort, see, recognizing that, oh, maybe I'm...

300
00:27:19,643 --> 00:27:21,264
[SPEAKER_00]: I was wrong, is unpleasant.

301
00:27:21,924 --> 00:27:24,865
[SPEAKER_00]: And now, the doubt comes on top of that, amplifies that discomfort.

302
00:27:26,781 --> 00:27:31,424
[SPEAKER_00]: Do you stay with it, and not fuel that kind of mental proliferation account without?

303
00:27:31,904 --> 00:27:36,767
[SPEAKER_00]: Or do you think, I must find a solution, while in reality, you're really just trying to avoid that very discomfort?

304
00:27:48,333 --> 00:27:54,577
[SPEAKER_04]: No, I think I would like to find a solution, but it feels like there's no solution, indeed, because I know

305
00:27:55,353 --> 00:28:00,314
[SPEAKER_04]: Well, you don't need to...

306
00:28:00,354 --> 00:28:15,018
[SPEAKER_00]: I mean, obviously, you can't know that there is liberation unless you're on path to be liberated yourself.

307
00:28:16,098 --> 00:28:24,780
[SPEAKER_00]: So... but then my point is, if you feel like you're losing faith, well, my point is that you also can't not... well, you can't know that there is no liberation either.

308
00:28:25,799 --> 00:28:26,820
[SPEAKER_00]: It's on the equal footing.

309
00:28:27,901 --> 00:28:35,367
[SPEAKER_00]: So, whether you want it or not, you are left in the state of a liberation, could be possible, and you have nothing to lose by trying.

310
00:28:36,528 --> 00:28:46,235
[SPEAKER_00]: Because your life is objectively better, by not pursuing career, family, worldly craving, money, partnerships, relationships, entanglement, burden, and so on.

311
00:28:47,396 --> 00:28:50,859
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you realize, okay, I...

312
00:28:51,854 --> 00:28:52,895
[SPEAKER_00]: I have a crisis of faith.

313
00:28:52,935 --> 00:28:55,957
[SPEAKER_00]: I don't know if it's possible, but I also don't know if it's not possible either.

314
00:28:56,258 --> 00:29:05,805
[SPEAKER_00]: Which means, I might as well continue doing what I can have, and intelligent recognition is beneficial for me, and carries the possibility of actually freeing me from suffering.

315
00:29:06,486 --> 00:29:15,874
[SPEAKER_00]: But at the same time, yes, you obviously need to develop some shift or some upgrade, if it's been 10 or more years, and you're still not free from suffering.

316
00:29:17,335 --> 00:29:20,317
[SPEAKER_00]: Not just indefinitely, well, it will come when it comes.

317
00:29:20,357 --> 00:29:20,878
[SPEAKER_00]: No, no, no.

318
00:29:21,841 --> 00:29:26,062
[SPEAKER_00]: Surely, in 10 years' time, if things were being done rightly, it should have come.

319
00:29:26,702 --> 00:29:27,902
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, it should have been developed.

320
00:29:30,003 --> 00:29:31,383
[SPEAKER_00]: And now, that's the unpleasant part.

321
00:29:33,483 --> 00:29:35,524
[SPEAKER_00]: You feel like you might have to start all over again.

322
00:29:36,164 --> 00:29:38,144
[SPEAKER_00]: Or you might have to undo what you thought you've built.

323
00:29:39,045 --> 00:29:40,285
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's where the doubt comes in.

324
00:29:40,685 --> 00:29:50,467
[SPEAKER_00]: But instead of taking it on, and not losing perspective, people then want to manage the doubt, one way or the other, to avoid the discomfort that it revealed.

325
00:29:58,757 --> 00:30:05,161
[SPEAKER_00]: But the good thing is, like, if you start practicing rightly, you don't have to wait for 10 years to get the results.

326
00:30:06,602 --> 00:30:17,770
[SPEAKER_00]: So, even if somebody, say, wasted 50 years of not getting anywhere, not getting anything from the practice, if then they start rightly, they can get it right.

327
00:30:19,231 --> 00:30:23,473
[SPEAKER_00]: So, from that point of view, nothing's been wasted, even the wrong effort, so to speak.

328
00:30:23,493 --> 00:30:27,216
[SPEAKER_00]: In hindsight, can all be made right, if things are actually then done rightly.

329
00:30:31,180 --> 00:30:41,384
[SPEAKER_00]: But until then, a person has to stop running away from confinement, internal confinement.

330
00:30:45,185 --> 00:30:46,205
[SPEAKER_00]: And that happens all the time.

331
00:30:46,245 --> 00:30:52,047
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, in most of the monasteries, I remember, it was like monks are constantly moving from one place to the other.

332
00:30:53,288 --> 00:30:59,530
[SPEAKER_00]: When things become, like, too close, too confining, too this, too that, go to the next place.

333
00:31:00,084 --> 00:31:01,125
[SPEAKER_00]: Go and find another ājā.

334
00:31:01,185 --> 00:31:02,066
[SPEAKER_00]: Go and find another one.

335
00:31:02,106 --> 00:31:03,006
[SPEAKER_00]: Go and find another one.

336
00:31:03,026 --> 00:31:03,827
[SPEAKER_00]: Go and find another one.

337
00:31:04,247 --> 00:31:07,550
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, I met marks of doing that for like 12, 13 years, 15 years.

338
00:31:08,871 --> 00:31:10,012
[SPEAKER_00]: And eventually, you get tired.

339
00:31:10,032 --> 00:31:12,974
[SPEAKER_00]: You can't be constantly moving or trying to outrun the confinement.

340
00:31:13,855 --> 00:31:15,336
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, the only other option was left.

341
00:31:15,496 --> 00:31:16,277
[SPEAKER_00]: Dismiss the whole thing.

342
00:31:17,137 --> 00:31:18,238
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, disrobe, basically.

343
00:31:18,278 --> 00:31:18,639
[SPEAKER_00]: Why?

344
00:31:18,719 --> 00:31:21,241
[SPEAKER_00]: Stop practicing.

345
00:31:21,381 --> 00:31:29,067
[SPEAKER_00]: And, eh... Again, all of that is the same attitude that's been maintained from the day one.

346
00:31:30,645 --> 00:31:44,098
[SPEAKER_00]: When things got too confining, I gave in and allowed the mind to run away through doing this or that and going there and justifying management and so on.

347
00:31:46,040 --> 00:31:50,744
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, you might kind of have to if it's too much, but you don't need to lie to yourself about it.

348
00:31:52,579 --> 00:31:53,719
[SPEAKER_00]: You don't need to justify it.

349
00:31:54,140 --> 00:31:54,880
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, oh, it's too much.

350
00:31:54,940 --> 00:31:56,060
[SPEAKER_00]: This monastery is just too much.

351
00:31:56,220 --> 00:31:57,041
[SPEAKER_00]: I can't bear it here.

352
00:31:57,101 --> 00:31:57,701
[SPEAKER_00]: It's too strict.

353
00:31:57,761 --> 00:31:58,681
[SPEAKER_00]: It's too this, it's too that.

354
00:31:58,701 --> 00:32:00,602
[SPEAKER_00]: The mind is overwhelming me.

355
00:32:01,362 --> 00:32:02,062
[SPEAKER_00]: I need to change.

356
00:32:02,662 --> 00:32:04,443
[SPEAKER_00]: I want to go somewhere else when it's a bit easier.

357
00:32:05,904 --> 00:32:09,085
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, a person might do that, but they can still do it authentically, so to speak.

358
00:32:09,365 --> 00:32:16,067
[SPEAKER_00]: They can recognize, okay, I'm not ready for this level of confinement, so I'll lower it down a bit, but

359
00:32:16,677 --> 00:32:23,584
[SPEAKER_00]: There is no need to lie to yourself and present that as some sort of practice and say, oh, you can't practice here, nobody knows what they're doing here.

360
00:32:24,024 --> 00:32:28,149
[SPEAKER_00]: Even if that were true, none of that should be your personal problem.

361
00:32:29,890 --> 00:32:37,598
[SPEAKER_00]: But people use that, finding faults and stuff, so to justify them running out of the confinement that their mind started perceiving.

362
00:32:38,947 --> 00:32:49,971
[SPEAKER_00]: Until the novelty of the new place runs out, and it starts to feel stale and confining again, and then again a new place, and a new place, and a new place.

363
00:32:49,991 --> 00:33:01,754
[SPEAKER_00]: And ironically enough, then people become... monks become even more sensitive, and disturbed, more easily disturbed, by sights, sounds, smells, tastes, touches, than they were before.

364
00:33:03,075 --> 00:33:06,396
[SPEAKER_00]: Because the more you're trying to outrun the confinement, the bigger it gets.

365
00:33:08,621 --> 00:33:10,022
[SPEAKER_00]: Which means the faster you need to run.

366
00:33:10,522 --> 00:33:13,724
[SPEAKER_00]: Which means the quicker you'll be reminded of the stuff that you're trying to outrun.

367
00:33:16,506 --> 00:33:18,167
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, they speak too loudly here.

368
00:33:18,207 --> 00:33:19,508
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, the smell here is too bad.

369
00:33:19,568 --> 00:33:20,529
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, the food is like this.

370
00:33:20,929 --> 00:33:21,850
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, it's too many people.

371
00:33:21,890 --> 00:33:22,811
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, it's too little people.

372
00:33:22,851 --> 00:33:23,391
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, it's too high.

373
00:33:23,411 --> 00:33:23,811
[SPEAKER_00]: It's too low.

374
00:33:23,831 --> 00:33:24,292
[SPEAKER_00]: It's too wet.

375
00:33:24,332 --> 00:33:24,892
[SPEAKER_00]: It's too dry.

376
00:33:25,572 --> 00:33:26,413
[SPEAKER_00]: It's always gonna be something.

377
00:33:27,894 --> 00:33:36,680
[SPEAKER_00]: And if you don't look past that and see where is my intention rooted in wanting to do this or that, move or say, and it's like, oh,

378
00:33:37,575 --> 00:33:48,264
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, it's obviously rooted in compulsion, in pressure, in mind trying to get me to to release it back into the wilderness, to some extent, to ease, to allow it some sort of relief.

379
00:33:48,304 --> 00:33:49,745
[SPEAKER_00]: It's like, oh, that's the problem.

380
00:33:50,906 --> 00:33:52,708
[SPEAKER_00]: And wherever I go, I'll carry that problem.

381
00:33:53,408 --> 00:33:54,429
[SPEAKER_00]: I'll carry that attitude.

382
00:33:55,150 --> 00:33:56,571
[SPEAKER_00]: So, no place will be perfect.

383
00:33:56,591 --> 00:34:02,596
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, you can live with a hundred arahants, Buddha himself, and that person would still find fault with it.

384
00:34:04,017 --> 00:34:05,419
[SPEAKER_00]: Because all of that's done just to

385
00:34:06,182 --> 00:34:08,662
[SPEAKER_00]: Compensate the responsibility for the confinement.

386
00:34:09,283 --> 00:34:16,684
[SPEAKER_00]: And there was that whole, like, Ajahn Chah, that funny story of monks go around the monastery, whichever monastery they're in, they complain about it.

387
00:34:17,524 --> 00:34:23,365
[SPEAKER_00]: And he said it's like going around from a place to place carrying shit in your shoulder bag.

388
00:34:24,245 --> 00:34:26,966
[SPEAKER_00]: And wherever you get to, you complain that that place smells of shit.

389
00:34:28,066 --> 00:34:29,586
[SPEAKER_00]: Because you brought it with yourself.

390
00:34:31,346 --> 00:34:34,927
[SPEAKER_00]: And, yeah, that's like... accepting of that,

391
00:34:35,652 --> 00:34:48,322
[SPEAKER_00]: Even if you don't do anything else ever in your life, just accepting that the value of not running away from the confinement imposed by the precepts restrained withdrawal, that's already a massive step in the right direction.

392
00:34:50,744 --> 00:34:55,928
[SPEAKER_00]: Because again, it's gonna be... the mind will not be indifferent towards it.

393
00:34:57,370 --> 00:35:00,392
[SPEAKER_00]: It's a very strong kind of habit and force.

394
00:35:00,992 --> 00:35:04,155
[SPEAKER_00]: They get people to move, or avoid, or compensate, or manage, or...

395
00:35:05,391 --> 00:35:08,933
[SPEAKER_00]: Develop the views as means of coping with that confinement.

396
00:35:09,473 --> 00:35:14,796
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you have to keep reminding yourself to stay with it, to not fuel it, to not blindly follow it.

397
00:35:16,837 --> 00:35:17,337
[SPEAKER_00]: How long?

398
00:35:17,518 --> 00:35:19,138
[SPEAKER_00]: Depends on how wild the mind was.

399
00:35:20,139 --> 00:35:23,501
[SPEAKER_00]: How wild you allow it... allowed it to be in the past.

400
00:35:25,302 --> 00:35:27,303
[SPEAKER_00]: But, sooner or later, it will have to sit down.

401
00:35:27,583 --> 00:35:29,624
[SPEAKER_00]: Same, again, simile of the elephant.

402
00:35:30,323 --> 00:35:33,024
[SPEAKER_00]: In the suttas, you trap it from the wilderness.

403
00:35:33,565 --> 00:35:37,407
[SPEAKER_00]: I actually saw a video of the ancient Indian technique of taming the elephant.

404
00:35:37,487 --> 00:35:38,927
[SPEAKER_00]: It was like what the suttas describe.

405
00:35:39,868 --> 00:35:46,371
[SPEAKER_00]: You get... you separate this elephant, you get them into this enclosure of this massive post and stuff, and they go feral.

406
00:35:46,391 --> 00:35:47,031
[SPEAKER_00]: They're mental.

407
00:35:47,091 --> 00:35:51,814
[SPEAKER_00]: They're trying to break, destroy, like absolute kind of rabbit panic.

408
00:35:53,335 --> 00:35:54,095
[SPEAKER_00]: But then they calm down.

409
00:35:55,461 --> 00:36:02,484
[SPEAKER_00]: They urinate, defecate, scream, but then there is like... the wall's too strong and then they calm down.

410
00:36:02,904 --> 00:36:03,825
[SPEAKER_00]: There is no other choice.

411
00:36:04,965 --> 00:36:06,646
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's exactly what the suttas describe.

412
00:36:08,027 --> 00:36:09,567
[SPEAKER_00]: The mind is contained.

413
00:36:10,148 --> 00:36:15,490
[SPEAKER_00]: When it calms down, then the monk kind of offers the next step of training, so to speak.

414
00:36:16,190 --> 00:36:18,491
[SPEAKER_00]: Or like the trainer for the elephant.

415
00:36:18,871 --> 00:36:22,313
[SPEAKER_00]: You confine it and you have to wait for it until it sits on its own.

416
00:36:22,984 --> 00:36:25,225
[SPEAKER_00]: Till it calms down, and then you offer it some food.

417
00:36:25,285 --> 00:36:26,285
[SPEAKER_00]: Okay, listen.

418
00:36:27,186 --> 00:36:33,128
[SPEAKER_00]: As it's clear to you now, we are not spilling out into the old ways that you are pressuring me to do.

419
00:36:33,748 --> 00:36:37,810
[SPEAKER_00]: But, if you're happy, we can do stuff within this new perimeter.

420
00:36:38,810 --> 00:36:43,712
[SPEAKER_00]: And if the mind says, no, get away, I'll kill you, then, okay, you wait there longer.

421
00:36:44,972 --> 00:36:51,275
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's exactly the part of the simile where the trainer comes and offers some food and water to the elephant that has calmed down because it exhausted itself,

422
00:36:52,414 --> 00:36:53,135
[SPEAKER_00]: But it's still wild.

423
00:36:53,155 --> 00:36:55,276
[SPEAKER_00]: It's like, okay, I'll come back later.

424
00:36:56,337 --> 00:36:57,458
[SPEAKER_00]: And then it comes back later.

425
00:36:57,938 --> 00:36:59,539
[SPEAKER_00]: Eventually, the elephant will accept it.

426
00:37:00,060 --> 00:37:02,442
[SPEAKER_00]: And when it accepts it, it's like, okay, now we speak on my terms.

427
00:37:05,564 --> 00:37:22,217
[SPEAKER_00]: And I think one of the... probably the entire, you know, what I always sort of have a bone to pick, the commentary, the contemporary views, the meditation techniques, the management, the worst thing of why all those things are so unbeneficial,

428
00:37:23,199 --> 00:37:32,464
[SPEAKER_00]: is because they kind of enable people to believe that they can start the training of the mind without the prior confinement.

429
00:37:34,825 --> 00:37:51,293
[SPEAKER_00]: Without having the mind settle down after the panic existential threat confined by the actual precepts being trained in, not giving in to the unbeneficial behavior, and then not running away from the confinement either, until you see your mind sit down.

430
00:37:53,086 --> 00:37:58,811
[SPEAKER_00]: And all these contemporary practices enable people to believe that they can practice without doing that.

431
00:37:59,032 --> 00:38:06,078
[SPEAKER_00]: Although, as I said, it doesn't take great kind of study of the suttas to discover that that is absolutely necessary.

432
00:38:06,419 --> 00:38:07,520
[SPEAKER_00]: That's like the first step.

433
00:38:09,241 --> 00:38:12,705
[SPEAKER_00]: And people will think, yeah, I know, I know, I'm doing it bit by bit.

434
00:38:12,845 --> 00:38:16,588
[SPEAKER_00]: In the meantime, the real practice is this or that, that, and I'm getting closer to it.

435
00:38:16,708 --> 00:38:17,149
[SPEAKER_00]: It's like, no.

436
00:38:18,966 --> 00:38:21,987
[SPEAKER_00]: You're just sort of believing that, yeah, look, the mind is listening, yeah, yeah.

437
00:38:22,407 --> 00:38:26,149
[SPEAKER_00]: Because the mind just happened to be wanting to walk in the same direction that you are going.

438
00:38:27,189 --> 00:38:31,250
[SPEAKER_00]: But, quickly you'll find out that it's not in your control when that direction changes.

439
00:38:32,751 --> 00:38:34,912
[SPEAKER_00]: Or when something else comes in the way and changes it.

440
00:38:38,513 --> 00:38:43,415
[SPEAKER_00]: So then, ironically enough, the practice of training the mind on account of the right confinement,

441
00:38:44,822 --> 00:38:51,884
[SPEAKER_00]: Get swapped for managing the mind and allowing it to perpetually, sort of, escape the confinement.

442
00:38:53,024 --> 00:38:59,486
[SPEAKER_00]: So, I don't go back to centrality, but I go back to this technique, to that teaching, to this place, to this place, move the monastery, move that.

443
00:38:59,866 --> 00:39:13,010
[SPEAKER_00]: So, basically, you... as I said, a person might not have been as, kind of, behaving coarsely about it, because they do keep the precepts, but now everything else they've done in the name of practice is actually rooted in avoiding confinement internally.

444
00:39:15,209 --> 00:39:18,311
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's why, like, they don't start practicing even 50 years later.

445
00:39:20,231 --> 00:39:23,253
[SPEAKER_00]: But it always remains perpetually available right there in your face.

446
00:39:25,634 --> 00:39:27,415
Did you say something?

447
00:39:38,834 --> 00:39:40,995
[SPEAKER_02]: Yes, it is like running away from suffering.

448
00:39:41,075 --> 00:39:44,057
[SPEAKER_02]: And I would like to know what suffering is.

449
00:39:44,477 --> 00:39:45,197
[SPEAKER_02]: Is it possible?

450
00:39:46,918 --> 00:39:47,038
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

451
00:39:47,058 --> 00:39:48,999
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, that's what suffering... that's where suffering comes from.

452
00:39:49,779 --> 00:39:57,043
[SPEAKER_00]: It comes from the... from the citta that has no diminished cravings.

453
00:40:05,907 --> 00:40:07,788
[SPEAKER_00]: And you don't see that presence of dukkha,

454
00:40:08,717 --> 00:40:10,858
[SPEAKER_00]: It's because of the presence of craving in your citta.

455
00:40:14,078 --> 00:40:16,299
[SPEAKER_02]: It is a raging elephant.

456
00:40:17,259 --> 00:40:17,459
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

457
00:40:17,739 --> 00:40:19,060
[SPEAKER_00]: It might not even be that raging.

458
00:40:19,320 --> 00:40:21,340
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, maybe you have kept the precepts.

459
00:40:21,440 --> 00:40:24,081
[SPEAKER_00]: Maybe you don't have strong desires.

460
00:40:24,101 --> 00:40:26,202
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, different people have different preferences and so on.

461
00:40:26,822 --> 00:40:33,964
[SPEAKER_00]: But the fact is, you haven't made a connection that presence of craving inside of the animal, inside of the mind, means presence for suffering for you.

462
00:40:34,124 --> 00:40:36,485
[SPEAKER_00]: Whether you get what you want or not, becomes irrelevant.

463
00:40:37,465 --> 00:40:37,825
[SPEAKER_00]: Simply,

464
00:40:38,185 --> 00:40:42,829
[SPEAKER_00]: The attitude of craving, in that elephant, is felt as suffering in you.

465
00:40:44,811 --> 00:40:49,575
[SPEAKER_00]: So that's why you want to train the mind to stop craving, so that you are free from suffering.

466
00:40:50,916 --> 00:41:01,404
[SPEAKER_00]: But for as long as you don't see that, your suffering will be attributed to, oh, because he said something, or I didn't get what I want, or I got too much of what I didn't want, and so on.

467
00:41:02,525 --> 00:41:04,207
[SPEAKER_00]: But those things are indirect,

468
00:41:06,012 --> 00:41:14,114
[SPEAKER_00]: Causes, so to speak, not even causes, they're indirect circumstances, because of which the animal chooses to react this way or that way, and you not see that.

469
00:41:14,394 --> 00:41:16,814
[SPEAKER_00]: By not seeing that, you are the one suffering on account of it.

470
00:41:18,214 --> 00:41:22,975
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why, like, the prerequisite for sotāpatti is seeing the animal, seeing the citta, to begin with.

471
00:41:24,356 --> 00:41:26,476
[SPEAKER_00]: Because that's where the craving is.

472
00:41:26,756 --> 00:41:28,236
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you are not doing the craving.

473
00:41:29,037 --> 00:41:30,957
[SPEAKER_00]: You're just inadvertently enabling it.

474
00:41:34,742 --> 00:41:37,164
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's also why you can't just stop, or just stop the craving.

475
00:41:37,224 --> 00:41:38,585
[SPEAKER_00]: Just let go and stop the craving.

476
00:41:38,785 --> 00:41:41,828
[SPEAKER_00]: Just switch it off.

477
00:41:41,848 --> 00:41:41,968
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

478
00:41:42,068 --> 00:41:46,011
[SPEAKER_00]: You gotta confine the animal so that it cannot proliferate that craving further.

479
00:41:47,072 --> 00:41:48,833
[SPEAKER_00]: And then it needs to calm down within that.

480
00:41:51,035 --> 00:41:59,381
[SPEAKER_00]: And then you dictate, as I said, you know, the beneficial behavior, the eightfold path, everything that, you know, the wholesome thoughts, not unwholesome, the right perceptions, and so on.

481
00:42:00,122 --> 00:42:05,088
[SPEAKER_00]: And then that craving of that animal, the urges, the compulsion, the pressure, become more and more apparent.

482
00:42:05,128 --> 00:42:09,914
[SPEAKER_00]: And the more apparent it is, the clearer it becomes to you that that's the root of suffering.

483
00:42:10,174 --> 00:42:11,896
[SPEAKER_00]: Absence of that is freedom from suffering.

484
00:42:13,237 --> 00:42:18,784
[SPEAKER_00]: And the path to fulfill that is the path of confining it rightly until it sees for itself.

485
00:42:22,512 --> 00:42:29,434
[SPEAKER_02]: And this contemplations of things, it is like food for this elephant.

486
00:42:29,634 --> 00:42:30,874
[SPEAKER_02]: When it is calmed down...

487
00:42:31,074 --> 00:42:39,537
[SPEAKER_00]: It's, yeah, same, like, well, you're familiar with the dog, so it's like, you know, you try to give a command to a dog that, that's not giving you attention, it's not gonna work.

488
00:42:40,917 --> 00:42:44,458
[SPEAKER_00]: So, first you have to get its attention, and then give it a command, and then it responds.

489
00:42:45,189 --> 00:42:45,889
[SPEAKER_00]: And this is the same.

490
00:42:45,929 --> 00:42:59,037
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if the mind is not confined enough, has not settled down in that confinement without you allowing it to spill out, because it was too much, because of your views took you in the directions and ideas that you know are rooted in compulsion.

491
00:43:00,078 --> 00:43:07,842
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if the mind is not calmed down enough, you giving perception, it's like shouting at a dog that not even see you, because it's so occupied with something else.

492
00:43:08,943 --> 00:43:10,424
[SPEAKER_00]: So, first you need to make it clear.

493
00:43:10,464 --> 00:43:10,924
[SPEAKER_00]: No, no, no.

494
00:43:11,684 --> 00:43:12,525
[SPEAKER_00]: You are here with me.

495
00:43:13,807 --> 00:43:17,710
[SPEAKER_00]: The world that you like, the wilderness that you depend on, is no more.

496
00:43:18,951 --> 00:43:32,101
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's why I say to people, like, even if you spend next 50 years trying to keep the eight precepts, and failing every second day, but each time you take it up, you take it in an infinite sense.

497
00:43:32,121 --> 00:43:42,249
[SPEAKER_00]: You are actually practicing more than somebody who keeps them for the rest of their life, but never really takes it on mentally, that for the rest of their life they will never engage with this.

498
00:43:42,733 --> 00:43:51,156
[SPEAKER_00]: They just circumstantially remain unengaged, but living off the pleasure of the possibility that they could escape the confinement if they want to.

499
00:43:52,576 --> 00:43:53,437
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's like, no, no, no.

500
00:43:53,517 --> 00:43:55,357
[SPEAKER_00]: It has to be infinite, even if you fail.

501
00:43:57,598 --> 00:44:02,040
[SPEAKER_02]: Yes, because this is talking through... Because that's what, exactly, that's what speaks to the mind.

502
00:44:03,200 --> 00:44:11,263
[SPEAKER_00]: When you start deciding or determining on the level of those possibilities of escape that the mind can find the joy in,

503
00:44:11,764 --> 00:44:13,706
[SPEAKER_00]: That's where the mind starts to listen.

504
00:44:15,167 --> 00:44:19,310
[SPEAKER_00]: But if it's just circumstantial, yes, no, I abstain this, I don't know, for how long?

505
00:44:20,831 --> 00:44:28,037
[SPEAKER_00]: It's like, okay, so the mind still lives off hope that, well, sooner or later, I can go back to those things.

506
00:44:29,358 --> 00:44:31,920
[SPEAKER_00]: And it's like, oh, it has to be other way around.

507
00:44:31,940 --> 00:44:32,961
[SPEAKER_00]: That's completely upside down.

508
00:44:33,121 --> 00:44:37,805
[SPEAKER_00]: It's like, even, as I said, even if you successfully manage to keep the precepts only for like one week,

509
00:44:39,172 --> 00:44:54,298
[SPEAKER_00]: If, when you decided to keep it with the idea was, oh, for the rest of my life, mind will not go back to this, that one week can be a lot more beneficial than, as I say, seven months of just keeping the precepts because you're in the environment when there is nothing challenging you and not many temptations.

510
00:44:54,819 --> 00:45:01,902
[SPEAKER_00]: But never take it on in that, like, mental sense, like, no, no, no, I am confining you within this new norm of behavior.

511
00:45:06,262 --> 00:45:20,474
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, yeah, if it's not indefinite, then the mind is like, sure, sure, sure, I'll listen to you until you let me out and then we're back onto, you know, what I used to do.

512
00:45:22,582 --> 00:45:24,764
[SPEAKER_01]: How do you balance that with, like, realism?

513
00:45:25,144 --> 00:45:38,977
[SPEAKER_01]: So, maybe not the precepts, but, for example, if you tell yourself, whenever the mind is lazy, like this reflection you were talking about, if you see your mind being lazy, you just do the thing that it's being lazy in regards to.

514
00:45:39,898 --> 00:45:47,605
[SPEAKER_01]: You can kind of take that on, but how would you do it in the permanent sense, if you just... if there's just kind of the realistic sense of knowing

515
00:45:48,385 --> 00:45:53,249
[SPEAKER_01]: That it will take several iterations of taking it on for it to stick.

516
00:45:53,809 --> 00:45:55,170
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, trial and error.

517
00:45:55,270 --> 00:45:55,490
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

518
00:45:56,911 --> 00:46:02,835
[SPEAKER_01]: But you shouldn't really... you shouldn't surrender to that, like, oh, I'll do it and then we'll see.

519
00:46:02,915 --> 00:46:07,718
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, no, because that already kind of dilutes the quality of it.

520
00:46:07,738 --> 00:46:07,959
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

521
00:46:08,159 --> 00:46:10,720
[SPEAKER_01]: That's already, like, half committed.

522
00:46:11,021 --> 00:46:14,423
[SPEAKER_01]: But it takes a bit more effort to re-establish it when you know that you've...

523
00:46:14,483 --> 00:46:14,883
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly.

524
00:46:15,003 --> 00:46:15,224
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

525
00:46:15,424 --> 00:46:15,804
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly.

526
00:46:15,904 --> 00:46:16,104
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

527
00:46:16,825 --> 00:46:17,205
[SPEAKER_00]: But see,

528
00:46:19,077 --> 00:46:26,120
[SPEAKER_00]: Through re-establishing it, the level where those determinations and decisions and choices should be made becomes clearer and clearer.

529
00:46:26,960 --> 00:46:27,520
[SPEAKER_00]: I see, yeah.

530
00:46:27,600 --> 00:46:30,381
[SPEAKER_00]: So you can find that spot, so to speak.

531
00:46:30,761 --> 00:46:31,422
[SPEAKER_00]: Quicker and quicker.

532
00:46:31,622 --> 00:46:31,742
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

533
00:46:32,082 --> 00:46:33,882
[SPEAKER_00]: Or more accurately, so to speak, yeah.

534
00:46:34,303 --> 00:46:40,385
[SPEAKER_00]: But it does always come down to... Again, none of these unbeneficial things can happen on their own.

535
00:46:41,485 --> 00:46:43,106
[SPEAKER_00]: They're always rooted in your choice.

536
00:46:43,726 --> 00:46:44,606
[SPEAKER_00]: It has to be that way.

537
00:46:46,047 --> 00:46:48,828
[SPEAKER_00]: So it's like, you are the one who chooses to release the elephant.

538
00:46:50,461 --> 00:46:56,243
[SPEAKER_00]: You don't have to let it go too far, to allow, to cover up, to pretend that it's not confined and so on.

539
00:46:58,403 --> 00:47:02,024
[SPEAKER_00]: Which means you could just abstain from that choice and maintain the practice.

540
00:47:02,244 --> 00:47:03,685
[SPEAKER_00]: You don't have to keep doing anything new.

541
00:47:15,148 --> 00:47:17,749
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's why everything boils down to, you know, what we often say in the talks,

542
00:47:18,918 --> 00:47:32,922
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, you know, you have the broad precepts, the kind of coarse behavior, kind of not giving in to that, but then the time comes when you recognize, am I acting out of pressure and do I feel justified in doing so?

543
00:47:32,942 --> 00:47:33,742
[SPEAKER_00]: It doesn't matter what it is.

544
00:47:36,523 --> 00:47:39,984
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, am I acting out of compulsion and do I feel justified in doing so?

545
00:47:40,784 --> 00:47:42,405
[SPEAKER_00]: Which means, I am giving in

546
00:47:44,450 --> 00:47:49,852
[SPEAKER_00]: To the degree that's still present in the... to the degree of wilderness that's still present in that elephant, and I'm giving in to that.

547
00:47:51,733 --> 00:47:58,176
[SPEAKER_00]: So, for as long as there is compulsion of any kind, craving of any kind, I can't say I'm free.

548
00:48:00,477 --> 00:48:05,859
[SPEAKER_00]: So, have I been inadvertently maintaining that craving through my views of what should be done in the name of practice?

549
00:48:09,561 --> 00:48:12,622
[SPEAKER_00]: While in reality, I was just acting out of compulsion and avoiding confinement.

550
00:48:16,091 --> 00:48:17,371
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's where the questioning comes in.

551
00:48:17,411 --> 00:48:19,552
[SPEAKER_00]: It's like, why do I need to do anything?

552
00:48:21,813 --> 00:48:24,074
[SPEAKER_00]: When the pressure arises to act, hold on.

553
00:48:26,775 --> 00:48:28,195
[SPEAKER_00]: Why do I accept it?

554
00:48:28,315 --> 00:48:29,395
[SPEAKER_00]: Why do I want to get rid of it?

555
00:48:29,936 --> 00:48:33,597
[SPEAKER_00]: Can I just not do either and stay with it for as long as it wants to stay?

556
00:48:33,937 --> 00:48:39,679
[SPEAKER_00]: Even if I hope, I hope it goes away soon, I still choose to stay with it and not make it go away.

557
00:48:43,640 --> 00:48:45,481
[SPEAKER_00]: Because having to do anything on account of

558
00:48:46,172 --> 00:48:48,152
[SPEAKER_00]: Anything is a form of burden.

559
00:48:50,153 --> 00:48:54,694
[SPEAKER_00]: Because what if you can't do what you think you're supposed to, or what you perceive there's the way out?

560
00:48:54,734 --> 00:48:55,514
[SPEAKER_00]: What if you can't do it?

561
00:48:57,654 --> 00:48:59,955
[SPEAKER_00]: So you can't then say, oh, I'm free.

562
00:49:00,055 --> 00:49:00,855
[SPEAKER_00]: I'm imperturbable.

563
00:49:00,935 --> 00:49:01,635
[SPEAKER_00]: I'm burdenless.

564
00:49:01,675 --> 00:49:06,436
[SPEAKER_00]: I'm untouched, like a lotus above the water, if I still have to do something.

565
00:49:07,696 --> 00:49:13,617
[SPEAKER_00]: Means I'm not... my mind is not calm enough or trained well enough in regard to the confinement, whereby

566
00:49:15,582 --> 00:49:18,183
[SPEAKER_00]: It can sit and take it, like the suttas describe.

567
00:49:18,343 --> 00:49:21,044
[SPEAKER_00]: The elephant getting it ready for the battle.

568
00:49:21,064 --> 00:49:24,165
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not being pronged with sticks and spears and hit.

569
00:49:24,825 --> 00:49:27,446
[SPEAKER_00]: And if it's still kind of shy, it pulls away, hides its trunk.

570
00:49:27,466 --> 00:49:28,386
[SPEAKER_00]: It's like, oh, it's not ready.

571
00:49:29,026 --> 00:49:30,187
[SPEAKER_00]: It's ready when it doesn't move.

572
00:49:31,347 --> 00:49:32,507
[SPEAKER_00]: Because it doesn't need to move.

573
00:49:34,588 --> 00:49:37,509
[SPEAKER_00]: But the only way to find that out is if you stop enabling it to move.

574
00:50:01,577 --> 00:50:14,141
[SPEAKER_03]: I think it's challenging when it starts accumulating, like with practicing, this comes, this comes, and then it just kind of goes on and then at one point you're just overwhelmed.

575
00:50:14,241 --> 00:50:14,502
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

576
00:50:14,882 --> 00:50:16,982
[SPEAKER_03]: But then it's again just... Then you restart.

577
00:50:18,323 --> 00:50:20,804
[SPEAKER_00]: And each time you restart rightly,

578
00:50:23,285 --> 00:50:27,187
[SPEAKER_00]: Being overwhelmed will be put in a better perspective.

579
00:50:28,967 --> 00:50:30,048
[SPEAKER_00]: Because that's the point.

580
00:50:30,148 --> 00:50:35,710
[SPEAKER_00]: It doesn't matter how many moving parts there are, the framework of the feeling remains the same.

581
00:50:36,890 --> 00:50:44,273
[SPEAKER_00]: So, whether it's one thing that's uncomfortable, or a thousand things uncomfortable, you know that discomfort is there.

582
00:50:44,293 --> 00:50:46,253
[SPEAKER_00]: That's the satipaṭṭhāna, correctly done.

583
00:50:47,094 --> 00:50:51,075
[SPEAKER_00]: Instead of this, from left I go to right, and do that, and when it's this, I press this, like, no.

584
00:50:51,877 --> 00:50:54,658
[SPEAKER_00]: I know pleasant feeling is pleasant feeling, unpleasant is unpleasant.

585
00:50:54,678 --> 00:50:56,739
[SPEAKER_00]: Whichever feeling is there, I know that's what's felt.

586
00:50:58,220 --> 00:51:00,841
[SPEAKER_00]: It doesn't matter if it's big or small, multiple, individual.

587
00:51:01,261 --> 00:51:03,102
[SPEAKER_00]: Same with the state of mind.

588
00:51:03,142 --> 00:51:06,664
[SPEAKER_00]: It doesn't matter why and how and how many times and how long.

589
00:51:07,364 --> 00:51:09,805
[SPEAKER_00]: It says he knows the mind of lust as mind of lust.

590
00:51:09,866 --> 00:51:10,326
[SPEAKER_00]: Full stop.

591
00:51:10,606 --> 00:51:11,886
[SPEAKER_00]: You don't need more information.

592
00:51:12,567 --> 00:51:14,848
[SPEAKER_00]: You don't need more specific details.

593
00:51:15,506 --> 00:51:16,967
[SPEAKER_00]: Mind of anger is mind of anger.

594
00:51:17,007 --> 00:51:19,488
[SPEAKER_00]: Doesn't matter if it's a little bit irritation or a big anger.

595
00:51:19,929 --> 00:51:20,969
[SPEAKER_00]: Mind of anger is there.

596
00:51:21,169 --> 00:51:21,890
[SPEAKER_00]: Animal is angry.

597
00:51:22,130 --> 00:51:22,810
[SPEAKER_00]: That's what I know.

598
00:51:22,950 --> 00:51:24,171
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's all I need to know.

599
00:51:26,833 --> 00:51:28,434
[SPEAKER_00]: Depressed mind as depressed mind.

600
00:51:29,514 --> 00:51:33,156
[SPEAKER_00]: Not depressed mind, but then you undepress it through doing this and that.

601
00:51:33,216 --> 00:51:33,377
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

602
00:51:33,637 --> 00:51:37,819
[SPEAKER_00]: Knowing it for what it is, is how you basically set it up rightly.

603
00:51:40,521 --> 00:51:42,702
[SPEAKER_00]: So, depressed mind is not a problem.

604
00:51:43,150 --> 00:51:44,371
[SPEAKER_00]: Mind of lust is not a problem.

605
00:51:44,411 --> 00:51:45,632
[SPEAKER_00]: Mind of anger is not a problem.

606
00:51:46,372 --> 00:51:50,995
[SPEAKER_00]: You not seeing it as such, that's where the problem is.

607
00:51:51,776 --> 00:52:00,161
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, and you don't see it because you keep giving in to the pressure and compulsion of tendency to just change it this one time and then I'll stop doing, I promise.

608
00:52:05,683 --> 00:52:15,326
[SPEAKER_03]: One thing I was thinking about the feeling, like there are three types of feelings, and me not being over the shore yet, means I have craving.

609
00:52:16,226 --> 00:52:17,007
[SPEAKER_00]: Not being over what?

610
00:52:18,167 --> 00:52:19,847
[SPEAKER_03]: Over the river, on the other side, right?

611
00:52:20,568 --> 00:52:31,071
[SPEAKER_03]: So, craving is always present, and craving, it's always felt unpleasantly, because it's... Well, yeah, yeah.

612
00:52:31,191 --> 00:52:34,512
[SPEAKER_00]: If a person's honest enough to admit that, and not just perpetually

613
00:52:35,271 --> 00:52:36,692
[SPEAKER_00]: Kind of maintain the delusion.

614
00:52:36,912 --> 00:52:37,152
[SPEAKER_03]: Yes.

615
00:52:37,172 --> 00:52:39,454
[SPEAKER_03]: So, that's what I was thinking.

616
00:52:39,534 --> 00:52:44,076
[SPEAKER_03]: Is then, everything that it feels pleasantly, actually not.

617
00:52:44,697 --> 00:52:45,918
[SPEAKER_03]: Like, it's kind of... Well, yes.

618
00:52:46,018 --> 00:52:46,198
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

619
00:52:46,318 --> 00:52:46,918
[SPEAKER_03]: Twisted.

620
00:52:47,539 --> 00:52:49,200
[SPEAKER_03]: Because of this... Well, yeah.

621
00:52:49,220 --> 00:52:57,225
[SPEAKER_00]: Because, like, when you get the elephant in the enclosure or the dog, everything he wants to do is for the sake of getting out.

622
00:52:59,426 --> 00:53:04,409
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's why there is no other way around or a magical solution other than

623
00:53:05,170 --> 00:53:12,172
[SPEAKER_00]: You have to keep it in that setup, in that internal confinement, until it calms down within that, and accepts that as the new environment.

624
00:53:13,192 --> 00:53:19,914
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, you can allow it to move, to do this or that, within that space, that's not concerned with escaping anymore.

625
00:53:21,454 --> 00:53:27,656
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's when you start to recognize the difference between intentions that are free from craving, and intentions that are not.

626
00:53:29,236 --> 00:53:31,897
[SPEAKER_00]: And intentions that are not, are the ones that are concerned with escape.

627
00:53:33,580 --> 00:53:34,540
[SPEAKER_00]: Escaping the confinement.

628
00:53:35,020 --> 00:53:37,881
[SPEAKER_00]: If the intention is not about escaping confinement, then it's fine.

629
00:53:39,022 --> 00:53:39,422
[SPEAKER_00]: That's what I mean.

630
00:53:39,442 --> 00:53:43,803
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, for as long as the dog wants to escape, well, you keep it on a leash.

631
00:53:45,183 --> 00:53:48,924
[SPEAKER_00]: Wants to jump the fence, wants to dig under it, you keep it on a leash.

632
00:53:49,764 --> 00:53:53,365
[SPEAKER_00]: When he comes down and stops doing that, you don't have to keep it, like, next to you.

633
00:53:53,385 --> 00:53:57,887
[SPEAKER_00]: You can let it go, let it run, let it play, because it's not concerned with escaping anymore.

634
00:54:00,207 --> 00:54:02,648
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, sure, you can refine that as well now,

635
00:54:02,970 --> 00:54:13,277
[SPEAKER_00]: That further training, but that will be self-evident, how to do it, if you would have overcome the general kind of tendency of the mind to want to escape all the way.

636
00:54:17,439 --> 00:54:19,080
[SPEAKER_03]: But that's the thing, if I...

637
00:54:19,200 --> 00:54:21,442
[SPEAKER_03]: I cannot know pleasant feeling because I have none.

638
00:54:22,983 --> 00:54:23,363
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, you do.

639
00:54:23,403 --> 00:54:24,384
[SPEAKER_00]: What do you mean you have none?

640
00:54:24,624 --> 00:54:25,284
[SPEAKER_03]: Because I have...

641
00:54:25,765 --> 00:54:32,329
[SPEAKER_00]: I keep having craving for... Well, yes, but again, craving is not the cause of feeling.

642
00:54:35,100 --> 00:54:36,402
[SPEAKER_00]: Craving is in regard to feeling.

643
00:54:37,303 --> 00:54:45,296
[SPEAKER_00]: So, everything you feel gets distorted through craving, but that doesn't mean that you don't know what pleasure or agreeability is.

644
00:54:47,599 --> 00:54:49,823
[SPEAKER_00]: You just don't know how to experience it apart from craving.

645
00:55:13,005 --> 00:55:14,466
[SPEAKER_00]: And think about, again, same similar.

646
00:55:15,667 --> 00:55:26,495
[SPEAKER_00]: You do have an animal enclosed, but still, what is on its mind, what is on your mind's mind, is to escape, to escape the confinement.

647
00:55:27,536 --> 00:55:32,700
[SPEAKER_00]: So, within that confinement, it can find agreeable things, but it won't appreciate them.

648
00:55:34,328 --> 00:55:42,930
[SPEAKER_00]: Because it's still concerned with more important thing to escape, because it still has a hope of escaping, because it still thinks that you will let it out eventually, maybe yourself are not clear, and so on.

649
00:55:44,331 --> 00:55:50,593
[SPEAKER_00]: But when it accepts that, that there is no escape from this confinement, and it calms down, then, yes.

650
00:55:51,533 --> 00:55:59,215
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, there is that joyful thing that I remember from before, I just paid no attention to it, because, again, my concerns were of a different kind.

651
00:56:02,927 --> 00:56:03,767
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's, you know, the same.

652
00:56:03,787 --> 00:56:04,628
[SPEAKER_00]: People can relate to this.

653
00:56:04,648 --> 00:56:08,209
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, you know, they're saying, oh, you don't know how good you had it until you lose it.

654
00:56:08,969 --> 00:56:13,411
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, things you take for granted, things you look past, things you think is irrelevant.

655
00:56:13,431 --> 00:56:14,492
[SPEAKER_00]: Give me that, give me that.

656
00:56:18,833 --> 00:56:19,353
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's what I mean.

657
00:56:19,393 --> 00:56:19,734
[SPEAKER_00]: There is.

658
00:56:20,094 --> 00:56:30,358
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's what Buddha says, like, develop pleasure of renunciation to abandon the pleasure of non-renunciation, the pleasure of non-confinement and spilling out into the sense world.

659
00:56:32,354 --> 00:56:34,615
[SPEAKER_00]: Same, develop the pains of renunciation.

660
00:56:37,116 --> 00:56:40,997
[SPEAKER_00]: So, to replace the pains of the world, the sensuality, and so on.

661
00:56:41,417 --> 00:56:44,718
[SPEAKER_00]: They're factually better pains, pains of renunciation.

662
00:56:44,738 --> 00:56:51,541
[SPEAKER_00]: So, pains that you experience on account of rightly set up confinement, will make you overcome the pains of the world.

663
00:56:52,841 --> 00:56:54,702
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, these pains will be easily understood.

664
00:56:55,531 --> 00:56:59,534
[SPEAKER_00]: Because they are not manifold, overwhelming, deceiving and so on.

665
00:57:00,054 --> 00:57:10,402
[SPEAKER_00]: Because the animal is contained and it's actually now there is a communication between you two, as opposed to open the gate and... you hope it might come back in three days, for a minute.

666
00:57:11,322 --> 00:57:17,747
[SPEAKER_00]: And generally just run on autopilot, in regard to wants, likes, dislikes, pressures, hindrances and stuff.

667
00:57:25,095 --> 00:57:25,698
Yes.

